Name: useSystemWindowColor

Type: property

Syntax: set the useSystemWindowColor to {true | false}

Syntax: get the useSystemWindowColor

Summary:
Specifies whether <stack|stacks> on macOS are drawn in the system's
light grey window colour instead of white.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, html5

Platforms: desktop

Example:
set the useSystemWindowColor to true

Example:
if the platform is "MacOS" then set the useSystemWindowColor to true

Value (bool):
The <useSystemWindowColor> is true or false. By default, it is false.

Description:
Use the <useSystemWindowColor> property to give the <stack|stacks> of an
application on macOS the light grey of the Mac's own windows.

On macOS, a <stack> whose <backgroundColor> is not set is drawn in white
in light mode, as it always has been, and in the system's dark window
colour in dark mode. When the <useSystemWindowColor> is true, such a
<stack> is drawn in the system's light grey window colour in light mode
instead of white; in dark mode it looks the same either way, and the
<stack> follows the change when the user switches between the two. A <stack>, <card> or
<control> with a <backgroundColor> of its own keeps it.

Setting the <useSystemWindowColor> redraws every open <stack>. It is a
global property: it is not saved with a <stack>, so an application that
wants it sets it when it starts, for example in its <openStack>
<handler>. The development environment sets it from the "Stacks in the
Mac's window colour" setting in the Appearance pane of its Preferences.

On Windows and Linux, a <stack> whose <backgroundColor> is not set is
already drawn in the system's window colour, and the
<useSystemWindowColor> changes nothing there.

References: backgroundColor (property), systemAppearance (property),
systemAppearanceChanged (message), openStack (message)

Tags: ui
